## Running it again

Running the migration again is safe. Each customer’s credit carries a unique key per source, so every balance imports at most once, whatever happens in between. After a run, the card’s button reads **Run again (imports only what is missing)**. A new run picks up:

- customers who appeared in the old plugin since the last run;
- customers whose anomalies you fixed at the source;
- the remainder of a run that was interrupted.

**Important:** A customer whose balance was already imported is never imported again, even if their balance in the old plugin changed afterwards. If you left the old plugin running after a migration and balances moved, correct those customers with a manual credit or debit. This is one more reason to deactivate the old plugin as part of the migration.

## Step 4: See what the customer sees

Open a private browser window, log in as the test customer and go to **My Account > Wallet**. The page shows:

- the **Balance**, and **Available to spend** when part of it is reserved;
- a **Transactions** tab with the same history you saw in the admin, in customer wording. Your credit appears as **Balance adjustment**. Staff reasons and notes on manual adjustments are never shown to customers;
- an **Add funds** tab, because top-ups are on by default;
- a **Withdraw** tab only if you enabled withdrawals, and an **Earlier activity** tab only after a migration from another wallet plugin.

![The customer's My Account Wallet page showing Balance, Available to spend, a Cashback on the way line, the tabs Transactions, Add funds, Withdraw and Earlier activity, and a table of transactions with Date, Activity, Amount and Balance](https://woocommerce.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/elite-balance-myaccount-transactions.png?w=980)My Account > Wallet on a store that also uses withdrawals, cashback and a migration.

## Deactivating

Deactivating never deletes anything. Wallets, the ledger, holds, withdrawal requests, earning rules and settings all stay in the database. While Elite Balance is inactive:

- the wallet payment method and the **Use my wallet balance** option disappear from checkout;
- the **Wallet** page disappears from My Account;
- no rewards are awarded and no wallet emails are sent.

**Important:** Wallet money reserved for an order that was partly paid from the wallet is collected only when Elite Balance sees that order get paid. Before you deactivate on a live store, let open orders that use the wallet get paid or cancel them. Reactivating brings everything back as it was.

## Allow Shop Managers to edit an additional role

This example adds the role with the slug 
```
subscriber
```

 to the default Customer allowlist. Use this only if that role is registered on your site, and confirm that its capabilities are appropriate. Keep the existing Customer entry unless you intend to remove it.

```
add_filter(
	'woocommerce_shop_manager_editable_roles',
	function ( $roles ) {
		$roles[] = 'subscriber';
		return array_unique( $roles );
	}
);
```

Install the snippet with a site-specific code-management method that runs in production only after it has passed review on staging. Do not add a role merely to make a particular account editable: first confirm that every capability granted by that role is appropriate for Shop Managers to manage.

### Detach the purchase order number display

The same callback is attached to three separate display hooks, so removal must name the specific hook. The admin class is a singleton created during plugins_loaded at priority 
```
10
```

, and remove_action must match the instance that was attached. The accessor function 
```
Woocommerce_Gateway_Purchase_Order_Admin()
```

 returns that instance.

This example stops the purchase order number appearing in order emails, and leaves the admin and customer order screens untouched. Place it in a child theme’s functions.php or a site-specific plugin.

```
add_action(
	'init',
	function () {
		if ( ! function_exists( 'Woocommerce_Gateway_Purchase_Order_Admin' ) ) {
			return;
		}
		remove_action(
			'woocommerce_email_after_order_table',
			array( Woocommerce_Gateway_Purchase_Order_Admin(), 'display_purchase_order_number' )
		);
	}
);
```

**Verify:** Create a Purchase Order test order and preview an order email. Confirm the PO number is absent from the email and still present in the order admin and customer order details.

### Detach the transaction ID sync

This callback is attached at priority 
```
99
```

, so remove_action must pass 
```
99
```

 explicitly. Removing it stops edits to the Transaction ID field on the order edit screen from writing back to the purchase order number.

```
add_action(
	'init',
	function () {
		if ( ! function_exists( 'Woocommerce_Gateway_Purchase_Order_Admin' ) ) {
			return;
		}
		remove_action(
			'woocommerce_process_shop_order_meta',
			array( Woocommerce_Gateway_Purchase_Order_Admin(), 'update_po_number_from_transaction_id' ),
			99
		);
	}
);
```

**Verify:** On a test order, change the Transaction ID and save. Reload the order and confirm that its purchase order number retains the previous value. Remove this customization before testing the default sync again.

### Detach the order confirmation message

The 
```
thank_you callback
```

 is attached by the gateway instance, which WooCommerce creates when it loads payment gateways. Retrieve that instance from the payment gateways registry rather than constructing a new one.

```
add_action(
	'wp',
	function () {
		if ( ! function_exists( 'WC' ) ) {
			return;
		}
		$gateways = WC()->payment_gateways()->payment_gateways();
		if ( isset( $gateways['woocommerce_gateway_purchase_order'] ) ) {
			remove_action(
				'woocommerce_thankyou_woocommerce_gateway_purchase_order',
				array( $gateways['woocommerce_gateway_purchase_order'], 'thank_you' )
			);
		}
	}
);
```

**Verify:** Place a Purchase Order test order. Confirm that the configured Order Confirmation Message is absent from its order-received page; the order and PO number should still be recorded.

### Remove the gateway entirely

This example removes the PHP gateway from WooCommerce’s registry and unregisters its block checkout integration. It also removes the gateway from **WooCommerce > Settings > Payments**. If you only need to stop accepting purchase orders, disable the gateway in its settings instead. This customization does not remove existing order data or saved settings.

```
add_filter(
	'woocommerce_payment_gateways',
	function ( $gateways ) {
		return array_filter(
			$gateways,
			function ( $gateway ) {
				return 'Woocommerce_Gateway_Purchase_Order' !== $gateway;
			}
		);
	},
	20
);

add_action(
	'woocommerce_blocks_payment_method_type_registration',
	function ( $registry ) {
		if ( $registry->is_registered( 'woocommerce_gateway_purchase_order' ) ) {
			$registry->unregister( 'woocommerce_gateway_purchase_order' );
		}
	},
	20
);
```

**Verify:** After applying both callbacks, confirm that Purchase Order is absent from classic checkout, block checkout, and Payments settings. Check the site’s error log for missing-gateway warnings. Remove both callbacks to restore registration.

